.TH SUNBENCH 1 "22 November 2026" "sunbench 2026.11.22-4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sunbench \- disk, CPU, memory and network benchmark for SunOS 4 and Solaris 2.x on SPARC
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sunbench
[
.I options
] [
.B disk
|
.B cpu
|
.B mem
|
.B net
|
.B full
|
.B menu
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sunbench
measures disk, CPU, memory and network performance on classic Sun hardware.
It is small,
self-contained (libc only \- no ncurses, no autoconf) and builds with the
bundled K&R
.BR cc (1)
on SunOS 4 as well as with an ANSI compiler on Solaris 2.x.
.PP
Results are honest because the page cache is bypassed.  On Solaris 2.6 and
later
.BR directio (3C)
is used, which makes the measurement independent of how much RAM the machine
has; on older systems the test file is instead sized larger than physical
memory.  The method actually in use is printed with every run.
.PP
Four access patterns are measured: sequential write, sequential read, random
read and random write.  Per-operation latency is accumulated into a
logarithmic histogram, so the distribution (not merely the average) is
available \- important on emulated SCSI devices, whose latency is often
bimodal.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B disk
Run the standard four-test benchmark at one block size.  This is the default
when a command is given.
.TP
.B cpu
Integer (ALU and multiply/divide), double-precision floating-point,
Linpack-style MFLOPS (single- and double-precision
.BR "y[i] += a*x[i]" ,
the Linpack inner loop, over a Linpack-100-sized working set AND over a
streaming footprint sized at run time to overflow any external cache --
on a big-cache machine the gap between the two is the cache benefit),
Dhrystone 2.1 and Whetstone kernels.  The mul/div kernel is separated from the
ALU kernel to expose the difference between sun4c (SPARC V7, software integer
multiply/divide) and sun4m/sun4u (V8/V9, hardware).  Dhrystone reports
Dhrystones per second and VAX MIPS; Whetstone reports MWIPS -- both for
comparison with published figures (note: Dhrystone 2.1 reports lower than the
older, more compiler-inflated Dhrystone 1.1 seen in vintage marketing).
Results are labelled with the build target (compiler and
.BR \-mcpu ),
since a V7 build and a native build measure different things on the same box.
Per-clock efficiency is reported when the clock is known (see
.BR \-\-mhz ).
On a terminal the CPU run is shown in the same live full-screen display as the
disk benchmark; piped, redirected or
.B \-\-plain
output stays plain text.
.IP
On machines with small direct-mapped instruction caches (for example
microSPARC), Dhrystone is sensitive to code placement: relinking the same
objects in a different order has been measured to change the score by almost
1.5x on a SPARCstation LX.  Compare machines using the same
.B sunbench
binary, and prefer the custom kernels (which fit in cache and are immune)
for exact cross-machine ratios.
.IP
With
.B \-\-mp
(or
.BR \-\-cpus )
the CPU test instead measures multiprocessor scaling: one process is run alone
for a baseline, then N processes run the same kernel concurrently (by
.BR fork (2),
never threads).  The report gives the single-process rate, the N-way aggregate,
the speedup and the scaling efficiency; efficiency below 100% reflects
contention on the memory interconnect between the processors.  MP figures are
wall-clock rates.
.TP
.B mem
Memory bandwidth and latency versus working-set size.  Five kernels --
integer read, 8-byte FPU read (on 32-bit SPARC
.B ldd
moves twice the bytes per instruction), integer write, libc
.BR memcpy ,
and a random pointer-chase reporting nanoseconds per dependent load -- are
each run over working sets from 1 KB up to min(256 MB, RAM/4), doubling each
step (see
.BR \-\-memsize ).
Bandwidth falls and latency rises at every cache boundary, so the table maps
the machine's cache hierarchy.  With
.B \-\-mp
(or
.BR \-\-cpus )
it instead runs five multiprocessor experiments, each pitting N concurrent
processes (private buffers) against one process alone: a 16 KB read as an
in-cache control (private L1 caches should scale at N.00x; less means the
run is noisy), read, write and
.B memcpy
copy at the full working-set size (write and copy add the coherency and
two-way traffic a shared bus or a crossbar handle differently), and the
pointer chase alone versus under N-1 streaming readers -- the loaded-latency
figure.  The aggregate-vs-single ratios show how much memory bandwidth the
interconnect has beyond a single CPU's demand, and what a busy neighbour
does to another CPU's memory latency.
.TP
.B net
Network benchmark against the bundled server,
.BR sunbench-netd ,
which runs on a modern machine (build it there with
.B make netd
from this same source tree) so the far end is never the bottleneck.
Everything rides plain TCP and UDP over IP, so any IP-capable interface --
Ethernet, token ring, FDDI, ATM, PPP -- is measured the same way, with no
link-layer code.  The tests: TCP upload and download (64 KB application
chunks, labelled in the report), TCP round-trip latency (ms), TCP
.B write-size and read-size
sweeps (throughput versus the application
.BR write (2) / read (2)
size, 64 bytes to 64 KB, in each direction -- these are not wire packets,
since the kernel segments to the MSS regardless; the curves isolate the
per-syscall cost that limits small-write programs on a slow CPU), and a UDP
datagram-size sweep in
.B both directions
reporting achieved Mbit/s
.B and
loss percentage per size.  The UDP download direction is paced by the server
to 1.5x the client's upload result for the same size, so its loss column
shows the client's receive ceiling rather than the raw speed mismatch with a
fast server.  The sweep is capped at the local interface's MTU
by default (detected via
.BR SIOCGIFMTU ;
Ethernet 1500, FDDI 4352, token ring and ATM larger), so each interface is
driven with the frame sizes it was built for;
.B \-\-netmax
raises the cap to explore IP fragmentation deliberately.
.TP
.B full
Unattended full suite: the standard disk benchmark, the block-size sweep,
the CPU benchmark, the memory sweep and -- when
.B \-\-server
is given -- the network benchmark, in plain text, appending everything
to the log.  On a multiprocessor the CPU and memory MP scaling stages run
automatically after their single-process counterparts (the suite proper is
always single-process, so machines compare on identical terms); on a
single-CPU machine they are skipped.
.B \-\-cpus
forces a specific process count.  Suited to running over a serial console or telnet and walking
away.  With
.B \-\-csv
each module writes its own derived file (the schemas differ):
.I fleet.csv
becomes
.IR fleet-disk.csv ,
.IR fleet-cpu.csv ,
.I fleet-mem.csv
and
.IR fleet-net.csv .
.TP
.B menu
Interactive arrow-key menu.  This is the default when
.B sunbench
is started on a terminal with no command.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-path " FILE"
Scratch file used for the benchmark.  Default
.IR ./sunbench.dat .
This must be on a real disk; see
.B NOTES
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-size " MB"
Size of the test file.  The default is chosen automatically: a fixed,
RAM-independent size when
.B directio
is available, otherwise physical memory times 2.5 (so reads stay cold across consecutive tests without directio).  The value is capped to
half of the free space on the target filesystem.  A larger file makes the
random tests scatter over a wider region of the disk, which is more
representative of real-world random access; the block-size sweep therefore
defaults to 256 MB.  A note is printed when the file ends up 64 MB or smaller,
as a small random span may not reflect real-world behaviour.
.TP
.BI \-\-block " KB"
I/O block size for the standard run.  Default 64.
.TP
.BI \-\-time " SEC"
Duration of each timed test.  Default 8 seconds (4 per cell in a sweep).
.TP
.B \-\-sweep
Run a block-size sweep from 4 KB to 1 MB instead of the standard run,
reporting throughput and IOPS for each block size.
.TP
.B \-\-diskfull
For the
.B disk
command: run the standard four-test run AND the block-size sweep, back to
back, in plain text -- the full disk suite, exactly the two disk stages
that
.B full
runs, without the CPU, memory and network modules.  Both append to the
same log, CSV and HTML report.  Combine with
.B \-\-hist
for the latency distribution.  The natural command for characterizing one
disk configuration.
.TP
.B \-\-hist
Print the full latency distribution for the random tests, as an ASCII
histogram, in addition to the percentile summary.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " FILE"
Show the report on screen as usual and additionally append a copy to
.IR FILE .
Each logged run is preceded by a separator, a timestamp and a system synopsis
(uname, hardware model, RAM, filesystem, disk device, drive model, and the
UP network interfaces with their MTUs), so a
single log may accumulate runs from many machines.  Progress output stays on
standard error and never enters the file.
.TP
.BI \-\-csv " FILE"
Append one wide, spreadsheet-ready row to
.IR FILE .
For a
.B disk
sweep the row is system metadata followed by throughput in MB/s for every
block size; for the
.B cpu
command it is metadata, clock and build target followed by the per-kernel
figures (integer, floating-point, Dhrystone and Whetstone); for
.B mem
it is metadata followed by read/read8/write/copy MB/s and chase latency for
every working-set size.  Each command uses its own schema.  The header line is written only when the file is empty, so
rows from many machines accumulate into one table.
.TP
.BI \-\-html " FILE"
Write a self-contained HTML report of everything run this session: inline
CSS, no JavaScript, no external references, printable.  Sweep tables carry
proportional bars and are followed by inline-SVG line charts (throughput
versus block size, the memory cache cliff and latency steps, the TCP and
UDP size curves).  The file is rewritten after each completed run, so a
long session grows it incrementally.  Reports generated by older versions
can be upgraded with
.BR sunbench-htmlgraph
(build with
.BR "make htmlgraph" ),
which parses the sweep tables out of an existing report and injects the
same charts.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress all progress output and success notices on standard error; warnings
and errors still print.  Implies
.BR \-\-plain .
Intended for scripts and cron:
.B sunbench \-q \-\-log ... \-\-csv ... \-\-html ... full
produces its artifacts and nothing else.
.TP
.BI \-\-note " TEXT"
Free-text value placed in the CSV
.B note
column, for facts
.B sunbench
cannot discover (OBP revision, SCSI chip, drive replacement, and so on).
.TP
.BI \-\-passes " N"
Repeat each test
.I N
times and report the spread.  For
.B disk
this tames SD-card and flash-based SCSI emulators, whose write throughput is
erratic -- garbage collection and cache flushes make a single short
measurement unreliable, while reads stay stable; the on-screen table shows the
median and the CSV records min, median and max for every metric.  For
.B cpu
each kernel is repeated and the median rate is reported with a
.RB \(+- "" %
spread, which smooths the cache-sensitive run-to-run scatter of Dhrystone and
Whetstone.  A characterization run: implies plain output.  Default 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-mhz " N"
CPU clock in MHz for the per-clock normalization in the
.B cpu
report.  Auto-detected via
.BR psrinfo (1M)
when not given.
.TP
.B \-\-mp
For the
.B cpu
and
.B mem
commands, run the multiprocessor scaling test across all online processors
(detected via
.BR sysconf (3C))
instead of a single-process benchmark.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpus " N"
As
.BR \-\-mp ,
but run exactly
.I N
concurrent processes.  Implies
.BR \-\-mp .
.TP
.BI \-\-memsize " MB"
Maximum working-set size for the
.B mem
sweep.  Default: min(256, RAM/4) MB -- large enough to overflow the biggest
cache of any machine (modern last-level caches reach tens of megabytes)
while fitting a small machine's RAM.
.TP
.BI \-\-server " HOST"
Machine running
.B sunbench-netd
for the
.B net
test.
.TP
.BI \-\-netport " N"
Control port for the network test (default 5202).
.B \-\-port
is accepted as an alias.
.TP
.BI \-\-netmax " BYTES"
Maximum UDP payload for the network sweep.  Default: the local interface's
MTU minus IP/UDP headers.  Values above the MTU deliberately exercise IP
fragmentation.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Force plain text output, disabling the full-screen interface.  Implied
whenever output is not a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-menu
Force the interactive menu.
.TP
.B \-\-full
Equivalent to the
.B full
command.
.TP
.B \-\-keep
Keep the scratch file after the run instead of removing it.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH USER INTERFACE
On a terminal, runs are shown in a live full-screen display drawn with plain
ANSI/vt100 escape sequences; no
.BR curses (3)
library is required.  When standard output is a pipe or a file, or
.B \-\-plain
is given, output is ordinary text with progress written to standard error,
so scripting and logging are unaffected.
.PP
The time spent painting the display is measured and excluded from the
reported throughput, so the interface does not affect the results even on a
slow serial console.  Per-operation latency never included display time.
.SH NOTES
On Solaris,
.I /tmp
is a
.BR tmpfs (7FS)
filesystem backed by swap.  Benchmarking a file there measures memory, not
storage;
.B sunbench
detects this and warns.  Use
.BR df (1M)
to choose a filesystem on a
.I /dev/dsk
device.
.PP
Random I/O on emulated SCSI devices is frequently limited by per-command
latency rather than bandwidth, which shows up as an IOPS figure that is flat
across small block sizes.  The block-size sweep is the quickest way to see
where that limit gives way to bandwidth.
.SH EXAMPLES
Standard run against a real filesystem:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sunbench \-\-path /export/home/sb.dat disk
.fi
.RE
.PP
Block-size sweep with the latency distribution:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sunbench \-\-path /export/home/sb.dat \-\-sweep \-\-hist disk
.fi
.RE
.PP
Unattended full suite, logged and tabulated for a spreadsheet:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sunbench \-\-path /export/home/sb.dat \-\-log fleet.log \\
         \-\-csv fleet.csv \-\-note "OBP 1.0; BlueSCSI Pico" full
.fi
.RE
.PP
CPU benchmark, logged and tabulated, then a multiprocessor scaling run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sunbench \-\-log cpu.log \-\-csv cpu.csv \-\-note "SM61" cpu
sunbench \-\-mp cpu
.fi
.RE
.PP
Memory cache-hierarchy sweep, and the aggregate-bandwidth MP test:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sunbench \-\-log mem.log \-\-csv mem.csv \-\-note "SS10 2x HS150" mem
sunbench \-\-mp mem
.fi
.RE
.PP
Network test (first start the server on a modern machine:
.BR "make netd && ./sunbench-netd" ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
sunbench \-\-server modernbox \-\-log net.log \-\-csv net.csv net
sunbench \-\-server modernbox \-\-netmax 8900 net   # explore fragmentation
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ./sunbench.dat
Default scratch file; removed after the run unless
.B \-\-keep
is given.
.TP
.I /etc/mnttab
Consulted to map the scratch path to its disk device.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success and 2 if the command line could not be parsed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR df (1M),
.BR iostat (1M),
.BR directio (3C)
.SH AUTHOR
Written for benchmarking vintage Sun workstations and servers.
